Ischemic stroke
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Sudden focal neurological deficit, Hemiparesis/Hemisensory loss, Acute aphasia/dysarthria

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-021
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of profound right-sided weakness and severe speech impairment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an acute, sudden onset of dense hemiplegia and aphasia starting exactly 45 minutes prior. Immediate face-to-face assessment is mandatory to confirm an acute stroke syndrome, establish the exact time of onset, and expedite emergency transport for time-critical thrombolysis or endovascular thrombectomy, as this intervention cannot wait for standard hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "He just dropped his coffee cup, his face sagged, and now he can't say real words or move his right arm." (Spouse)
History of Present Illness: Patient was well until approximately 45 minutes ago when the spouse witnessed an acute, sudden onset of distinct focal neurological deficits. There was unilateral motor weakness presenting as sudden dense hemiplegia affecting the right face and arm, with the leg moderately affected. Concurrently, the patient developed acute expressive aphasia, firmly indicating dominant hemisphere cortical ischemia. The spouse notes profound right-sided hemisensory loss, as the patient did not react to a hot spill. There is an abrupt onset of severe dysarthria alongside a prominent right facial droop. There is a distinct absence of a severe, explosive thunderclap headache precisely at the onset of symptoms. The progression of the focal deficits has remained static since onset.
Functional Status: Acutely bedbound/chairbound; unable to communicate or mobilize.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate life-threatening neurological emergency; aspiration risk due to facial droop and potential dysphagia.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 185/100 mmHg | HR: 110 bpm (irregular) | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Alert but severely frustrated, unable to verbalize appropriately, right-sided neglect.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam confirms sudden focal neurological deficit mapping to the left middle cerebral artery (MCA) territory. Right-sided upper motor neuron facial droop (sparing the forehead). Dense right hemiparesis (Power: Arm 0/5, Leg 2/5) and right hemisensory loss to pinprick. Acute expressive aphasia is present; the patient follows simple one-step midline commands (e.g., "close your eyes") but cannot name objects or repeat phrases. Pupils are equal and reactive. Pulse is irregularly irregular, suggesting undiagnosed atrial fibrillation.
Environmental Observations: Spilled coffee on the floor and armchair; patient situated safely in a recliner.
Medication Reconciliation: Only takes a daily multivitamin. No known history of anticoagulation.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Ischemic Stroke (Left MCA territory, likely cardioembolic) - Status: Hyperacute
Differentials:

Hypoglycemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Point-of-care capillary blood glucose is 6.2 mmol/L.

Todd's Paresis (Post-ictal) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: No witnessed seizure activity, sudden onset of maximal deficit without preceding aura or motor convulsions, and no prior seizure history.

Hemorrhagic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of severe headache, explosive vomiting, or rapidly declining level of consciousness (though definitively requires CT to exclude).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents within the hyperacute window (under 4.5 hours) with a classic left MCA syndrome (aphasia, right hemiparesis, right facial droop) and a newly discovered irregular pulse suggestive of atrial fibrillation. Delaying assessment or relying on non-emergency transport would completely forfeit the narrow therapeutic window for intravenous thrombolysis or mechanical thrombectomy, committing the patient to devastating, lifelong disability. Urgent after-hours assessment was critical to rapidly perform a structured neurological exam (NIHSS components), secure the exact 'last known well' time, rule out stroke mimics like hypoglycemia, and mandate immediate, Priority 1 ambulance transfer with hospital pre-notification.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Activated Emergency Medical Services (000) for Priority 1 transfer with "Code Stroke" pre-notification. Kept patient strictly nil by mouth (NBM). Secured a large-bore peripheral IV cannula while awaiting paramedics.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Step 1: By strictly forbidding the patient from drinking water or taking any oral medications, the spouse actively prevents fatal aspiration into the lungs while the swallowing muscles are paralyzed.

Step 2: By immediately gathering the patient's ID, Medicare card, and the precise time the symptoms started, the spouse directly accelerates the hospital's triage process, saving critical minutes for brain-saving treatments.

Step 3: By remaining calm, maintaining eye contact, and speaking in simple, reassuring sentences, the spouse reduces the patient's profound terror caused by the sudden inability to communicate.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A - Immediate Hospitalization.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Handover to tertiary hospital Stroke Team.

